×¿Âõ<␀␀␀€R¸Ş¿áÑÆ<Êl¼R¸Ş¿Âõ<␀␀␀␀R¸Ş¿áÑÆ<Êl<R¸Ş¿␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¸å¿áÑÆ<Êl<R¸Ş¿3á<¿·é<R¸Ş¿␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¸å¿3á<¿·é<R¸Ş¿3á¼¿·é<R¸Ş¿␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¸å¿3á¼¿·é<R¸Ş¿áÑÆ¼Êl<R¸Ş¿␀␀␀€␀␀␀␀¸å¿áÑÆ¼Êl<R¸Ş¿Âõ¼␀␀␀␀R¸Ş¿␀␀␀€␀␀␀␀¸å¿Âõ¼␀␀␀␀R¸Ş¿áÑÆ¼Êl¼R¸Ş¿␀␀␀€␀␀␀␀¸å¿áÑÆ¼Êl¼R¸Ş¿3á¼¿·é¼R¸Ş¿␀␀␀€␀␀␀€¸å¿3á¼¿·é¼R¸Ş¿3á<¿·é¼R¸Ş¿␀␀␀€␀␀␀€¸å¿3á<¿·é¼R¸Ş¿áÑÆ<Êl¼R¸Ş¿␀␀␀␀␀␀␀€¸å¿áÑÆ<Êl¼R¸Ş¿Âõ<␀␀␀€R¸Ş¿␀␀␀␀␀␀␀€¸å¿# Bathhouse - a new room, and a template for making one

A Roman bath deep in the dungeon. Creatures of a keeper who has one go there
every six minutes, soak in the pool, mend and calm down; a keeper who could
build one and has none finds them souring. Everything it is lives in this
folder; copy it to start a room of your own.

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `mod.toml` | Its name, description and kind (`content`) in the Mods tab. |
| `game.toml` | The room (`[new_rooms.Bathhouse]`), its behaviour (`[rooms.*]` keys there), its floor's light (`[terrain.Bathhouse]`) and the need that fills it (`[needs.bath]`). |
| `Data/Meshes/BATHHOUSE<n>.kmf` | Its fifteen pieces, made by `source/make.zsh`. |
| `Data/Meshes/BATH_FOUNTAIN.kmf` | The steaming fountain that stands in a 5x5 bath where a Temple has its hand (`[new_objects."Bath Fountain"]`, `ritual_object`). |
| `textures/*.png` | Their textures, and its panel icon `gui_rooms_bathhouse.png`. |
| `source/pieces.py` | Draws the pieces (OBJ), in code: nothing taken from the game's own. |
| `source/make.zsh` | `pieces.py` -> `kmf_tool from-obj` -> `Data/Meshes`. |
| `source/prompts/` | The prompts the textures and the icon were made from, and how (`source/prompts/README.md`: the model, the game's pictures used as style references, `tools/image/` to finish them). |

The game reads `mod.toml`, `game.toml`, `Data/` and `textures/`; `source/`
and this file are for people.

## Making a room of your own from it

1. Copy the folder (`mods/<yours>`), rename the room in `game.toml`
   (`[new_rooms.<Name>]`, `title`, the `[terrain.<Name>]` and the need's
   `room`) and in `mod.toml`.
2. Pick its `base`: the original room whose layout and furniture it takes
   (the Bathhouse is laid out as the Temple: a ring of floor round a sunken
   middle). `works = true` would make it do the base's work too.
3. Its pieces: `art_from` is the base's piece prefix (`TEMPLE`), `art_to`
   yours; ship `Data/Meshes/<art_to><n>.kmf` for the pieces you make, the
   base's are drawn for the rest. `kmf_tool list <game> <prefix>` lists the
   base's, `kmf_tool to-obj` shows one in Blender (as a guide to size and
   placement: the base's own pieces stay out of a mod), `source/pieces.py`
   shows how each is drawn and where it sits.
4. Its look without new pieces: `tint` (its pieces and icon in a colour),
   its floor's `light` / `ambient` / `light_height_percent`.
5. Its centrepiece (a 5x5 room): `[new_objects.*]` with your mesh and
   `ritual_object` / `ritual_effect` in the room's keys.
6. What it does: a need that sends creatures there (`[needs.*]`: how often,
   how long, health, calm, which creatures), `wade_percent` and
   `ambience_seconds` for a pool. Water on a piece is best the game's own
   texture `Water0`: the engine draws it with its water shader.
7. Start a new level with the example from the checkout root (`GAME` is the original installation):

   ```sh
   python3 tools/native/run_level.py --game "$GAME" --level Level1 --mod bathhouse --all-rooms --gold 10000
   ```

   Select Bathhouse and build a 5Ã—5 room to see its pool and central fountain. The bath need is due after 360 seconds, not on placement. To pre-place a test area, use the host's `--room NAME X Y W H` (not a wrapper flag). For your own mod, substitute its folder name for `bathhouse`.

See the [mod guide](../README.md) for load order, file formats and keys.
# The Bathhouse: a new room (mods/README.md, "New rooms") and the need that fills it.
# It is laid out as the Temple it is made from, with pieces of its own - a Roman bath: marble
# floor and coping, columns round a warm pool over a mosaic, red fresco walls with niches - and a
# terrain of its own, so the game tells the two apart. Nothing of the Temple's own goes with it:
# no prayer, no mana, no sacrifices.
#
# Every creature of a keeper who has one goes there every six minutes or so, soaks a quarter of a
# minute, is mended a quarter of its health and comes out calmer. A keeper who could build one
# and has none finds his creatures slowly souring.

[new_rooms.Bathhouse]
base = "Temple"
title = "Bathhouse"
cost = 250                        # gold a tile (the Temple: 3000)
icon = "gui\\rooms\\bathhouse"    # textures/gui_rooms_bathhouse.png
art_from = "TEMPLE"               # its pieces are BATHHOUSE<n>: Data/Meshes/BATHHOUSE<n>.kmf in this mod,
art_to = "BATHHOUSE"              # made by source/make.zsh (a Roman bath)
drop = 0                          # a creature set down there just stands (the Temple's own: it prays)
lodging = 0                       # it draws no creatures through the portal
wade_percent = 45                 # in the pool they stand on its bottom (0.45 of a tile down), waist-deep
ambience_seconds = 0              # (the Temple's own pool mist and bubbles: its mist textures are missing, its bubbles a big glint - left off)
ritual_object = "Bath Fountain"   # where a 5x5 Temple raises its hand, a fountain stands in the pool
ritual_effect = "Workshop Steam"  # ... steaming (an Effects.kwd name)
ritual_effects_per_second = 1     # a thin plume (more and it piles up white)

[needs.bath]
room = "Bathhouse"
every_seconds = 360
stay_seconds = 15
heal_percent = 25
calm = 2500
anger_per_turn = 8                # while it is due and there is none to go to (a night in the lair clears it)
animation = 23                    # kneeling, as at prayer: in the water it reads as soaking (no creature has a sitting animation)
order = 60                        # after the original's four (and the Habits mod's)
interior = 1                      # in the pool, not on its coping

[new_objects."Bath Fountain"]      # the fountain: the Temple's hand's record, drawn with this mod's mesh
base = "Temple Hand"
mesh = "BATH_FOUNTAIN"            # Data/Meshes/BATH_FOUNTAIN.kmf (source/pieces.py)

[terrain.Bathhouse]               # its floor's light: a pale, steamy daylight off the water (the Temple's is a cold cyan glow);
light = [125, 148, 160]           # the water is lit by it too, and a warm light turned it to mud
ambient = [18, 20, 22]
light_height_percent = 215        # hung above the columns' capitals (the Temple's 150 lights them from below)
title = "Bathhouse"
description = "A new room, a Roman bath: creatures come to soak, mend and calm down - and grow sour without one."
kind = "content"
